                    briefing_text_r1:       `You have the full cost stack, function by function: the in-house tooling shop (<strong>S/ 11M/yr</strong> fixed, ~60% utilised), the two single-source motor SKUs (<strong>11-week</strong> lead time), and the fleet, IT and 60-seat call centre. Before you cut anything, classify each as <em>core capability</em> (a differentiator customers pay for), <em>commodity / context</em> (necessary but undifferentiated), or <em>strategic-but-fragile</em> (a resilience risk). The arc begins with diagnosis — separating what <em>feels</em> core from what <em>is</em> core. Get the tooling shop wrong here and every later decision inherits the error.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Make, Buy, or Bend`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Hit the <strong>S/ 47M</strong> target without breaking the design-iteration speed the anchor client pays for. For each function decide: <em>make</em> (keep in-house), <em>buy</em> (outsource for cost), or <em>bend</em> (dual-source, variabilise, or partially outsource). A flat 15% across everything is a haircut, not a strategy. Outsourcing the tooling core books S/ 4.5M and risks the <strong>S/ 166M</strong> renewal. Converting fixed context cost to variable is the operating-leverage move the covenant needs. What do you guarantee the anchor client?`,
