                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_toolcommodity: `<strong>You filed the tooling shop under “commodity.”</strong> It's the highest fixed-cost unit, true — but it's also the source of your quality reputation and the design-iteration speed your anchor client renews for. Treat it as a pure cost centre now and you'll “save” your way out of your differentiator.`,
                    coach_r1_motorcommodity:`<strong>The two motor SKUs are not a commodity — they're a single-point-of-failure.</strong> One Asian supplier, 11-week lead time, and a stopped line costs S/ 180,000 a day. Misclassify the fragility here and you'll under-fund resilience exactly where a disruption will hurt most.`,
                    coach_r1_contextcore:   `<strong>Trucks, IT and a call centre as “core”?</strong> They're necessary, but they don't differentiate you — customers don't renew because you own 28 trucks. Calling context “core” is how the fixed-cost base becomes untouchable and the covenant breaches.`,
                    coach_r1_blind:         `<strong>This classification is the opposite of the diagnosis.</strong> You've tagged the differentiator as disposable and the context as sacred. Every Round 2 decision now inherits the error — separate what <em>feels</em> core from what <em>is</em> core before you cut a single Sol.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> Tooling read as the core capability, the motors as the single-point-of-failure, the fleet/IT/call-centre block as context to variabilise. That's the map that lets you cut the S/ 47M from the right places and protect the differentiator at 0%.`,
                    coach_r2_flat:          `<strong>A flat 15% on everything is a haircut, not a strategy.</strong> You just shrank the tooling differentiator at the same rate as the call-centre overhead. Where is the cut supposed to come from disproportionately, and what do you protect at 0%? A haircut treats your edge and your fat as the same thing.`,
                    coach_r2_trim:          `<strong>You protected every function and trimmed discretionary spend.</strong> That finds maybe S/ 18M of the S/ 47M, and the covenant still breaches toward 3.3x. The bank doesn't care that you're loyal to your fixed-cost base. What gets variabilised — and why is “we make everything ourselves” a habit, not a strategy?`,
                    coach_r2_toolbuy:       `<strong>You booked S/ 4.5M outsourcing the tooling shop — and put S/ 166M at risk.</strong> When the anchor client's next design change takes the new partner six weeks instead of nine days, the renewal goes on hold. You saved S/ 4.5M to risk S/ 166M. Which one was the core capability?`,
                    coach_r2_toolmake:      `<strong>Keeping tooling 100% in-house protects the core — but books no saving.</strong> At ~60% utilisation it carries fixed cost you haven't touched. The point isn't keep-or-cut; it's whether you can <em>bend</em> it — variabilise the overflow while retaining the design-iteration core and the SLA.`,
                    coach_r2_toolbend:      `<strong>Bending the tooling shop is the move.</strong> A nearshore co-development partner takes the overflow and variabilises part of the S/ 11M, while you retain the design-iteration core and can still guarantee the SLA. You take cost out without forfeiting the capability the renewal pays for.`,
                    coach_r2_price:         `<strong>Matching the Mexican 8% underbid turns the renewal into a price fight you can't win.</strong> The client's clause is about service level <em>and</em> cost — meet the SLA from your retained capability and bring a credible cost number, don't just discount into a thinner margin.`,
                    coach_r2_anchornone:    `<strong>“Loyalty will carry the renewal” is a S/ 166M bet on goodwill.</strong> A competitor is quoting 8% lower and the client has a cost-and-SLA clause on the table. Guarantee something concrete — a design-iteration SLA your capability can actually back.`,
                    coach_r2_strong:        `<strong>That's the coherent plan.</strong> Selective, capability-weighted cuts; the tooling core bent rather than sold; context variabilised for operating leverage; and a design-iteration SLA the retained core can actually honour. The S/ 47M comes from the right places and the renewal is defensible.`,
                    coach_r2_hollow:        `<strong>You've promised an SLA you just sold the capacity to deliver.</strong> Outsourcing the tooling core and then guaranteeing design-iteration speed to the anchor client is a promise the new partner controls, not you. The guarantee is only as real as the capability behind it.`,
                    coach_r3_zero:          `<strong>Zero resilience spend with a force majeure already declared.</strong> You're maximising the cost number while the single-source supplier idles Line 2 for six weeks at S/ 180,000 a day — that's about S/ 7.5M gone, more than you'd save by skipping the budget. Where's the redundancy, and what was it worth?`,
                    coach_r3_overinsure:    `<strong>You dual-sourced and buffered everything.</strong> Working capital just ballooned and unit cost rose — you've over-insured. Resilience has a price; not every link is single-point-of-failure. Which two links truly are, and which are you protecting out of anxiety? Risk-tier it.`,
                    coach_r3_tiered:        `<strong>That's risk-tiered resilience.</strong> You dual-sourced and buffered the two true single-points-of-failure and underwrote the SLA with a nearshore partner — without blanket-insuring links that never needed it. Line 2 survives the force majeure and the budget did real work.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>The balanced case is what the client and the bank can both believe.</strong> Cost achieved, resilience posture, capability-retention guarantees, and a credible path back above 3.0x. You're not hiding a weak number behind a strong one — the four KPIs tell one coherent story.`,
                    coach_r4_cost:          `<strong>Leading with the cost number alone invites the obvious probe.</strong> “You hit S/ 47M — but can you still iterate a design in nine days?” The savings figure means nothing to the anchor client if the capability behind the SLA is gone. They'll want the resilience and capability story too.`,
                    coach_r4_loyalty:       `<strong>“Trust us, we've always delivered” isn't a renewal case.</strong> A rival is 8% cheaper and the bank wants a covenant path. Relationship buys you the meeting; the cost number, the SLA you can back, and the resilience posture win the contract.`,
                    coach_r4_model:         `<strong>A deliberately variabilised model is the durable answer.</strong> You've lowered the breakeven, protected the design-iteration core, and kept risk-tiered resilience — that eases the covenant in any downturn and isn't an identity sacrifice. The “bends” were strategy, not a temporary patch.`,
                    coach_r4_refix:         `<strong>Re-fixing the moment the Sol recovers throws away the operating leverage you just built.</strong> Converting fixed to variable lowered your breakeven and eased the covenant — snapping back to “we make everything ourselves” re-loads the fixed cost that caused the problem. Keep the leverage that earns its keep.`,
                    coach_r4_outsource:     `<strong>“Outsource everything for the lowest unit price” is TCO blindness.</strong> Unit price ignores switching cost, quality risk, coordination overhead and lost responsiveness — and it hollows out the very core capability the renewal pays for. Lowest unit cost is not lowest total cost.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Tooling = core, motors = fragile, context = commodity. Clean diagnosis.`,
