                    insight_resilient:      `Resilient and lean: you took the S/ 47M from the right places — selective, capability-weighted cuts — bent the tooling core rather than selling it, variabilised context for operating leverage, and bought risk-tiered resilience that kept Line 2 running. The anchor renewal is defensible, the margin recovered, and the covenant is back inside 3.0x. That is the make/buy/bend decision as a resilience question, not a cost-arithmetic one.`,
                    insight_corelost:       `Core outsourced: letting the tooling shop go booked a small saving and forfeited the design-iteration speed the S/ 166M anchor client renews for. You saved S/ 4.5M to put S/ 166M at risk — the headline mistake of the whole exercise. The tooling shop was context-priced but core-valued; cutting it scored as savings while it destroyed value.`,
                    insight_linedown:       `Line down: you under-funded resilience and the single-source force majeure idled Line 2. Redundancy is priced against disruption cost, not treated as waste — the line-down lost more than the resilience budget would ever have cost. The fragile single-source link was exactly where the Sol should have gone.`,
                    insight_overinsure:     `Over-insured: dual-sourcing and buffering every input ballooned working capital and raised unit cost on links that were never single-point-of-failure. Resilience has a price; not every link deserves the same protection. Risk-tier it — protect the two true single-points-of-failure, not your anxiety.`,
                    insight_flat:           `Flat haircut: an across-the-board 15% shrank the differentiator at the same rate as the overhead. That's not a strategy — it caps the capability you needed to defend the renewal. Selective, capability-weighted cuts protect the edge at 0% and take more from context.`,
                    insight_inertia:        `Identity inertia: keeping everything in-house protected every function and found only part of the target, while the covenant kept breaching. “We make everything ourselves” is a habit, not a strategy — the operating-leverage move (fixed to variable) was the one the bank required.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: parts of the plan landed but the cost, leverage, resilience and renewal pieces didn't fully reinforce each other — enough gaps for the board to question whether the recovery holds and the renewal is safe.`,
                    insight_target_miss:    `You did not reach the S/ 47M mandate: the board asked for 15% in two quarters and the gap keeps the covenant under pressure. The savings had to come from variabilising context, not from cutting the core or from discretionary trims alone.`,
                    insight_covenant:       `The covenant is still breached above 3.0x: EBITDA hasn't recovered enough and the bank's review and rating-downgrade clock is running. Operating leverage — converting fixed cost to variable — is the lever that pulls the ratio back inside the limit.`,
                    insight_linedown_cost:  `The single-source line-down cost real contribution margin — more than the resilience spend that would have prevented it. That is the exact arithmetic of pricing redundancy against disruption cost.`,
                    insight_save_vs_anchor: `You hit the cost target but only by outsourcing the core: the S/ 4.5M of tooling savings is dwarfed by the S/ 166M renewal you put at risk. Hitting the number is not winning if the differentiator that wins the contract is gone.`,

                    hint_corelost:          `You've outsourced the tooling core — capability is capped and the anchor renewal is exposed. There is no clean recovery from selling the differentiator; the lesson is that this was core, not context.`,
                    hint_linedown:          `The force majeure idled Line 2 — that line-down cost more than the resilience budget. Fund the single-point-of-failure first next time; redundancy is priced against disruption cost.`,
                    hint_risk:              `Anchor-renewal risk is dangerously high. Guarantee a design-iteration SLA you can actually back, and lead the board pitch with the balanced case, not one number.`,
                    hint_overinsure:        `You've over-insured — working capital and unit cost are up for protection you didn't need. Risk-tier it: protect the two true single-points-of-failure, not every link.`,
